"""Grade 8 life essentials: habits, prayer and loss."""
from spec import L, U

M8 = U('Habits, prayer and loss', 'Morality', 'How do I change a habit, pray in a way that works, and face loss with hope?', [
L('Changing a habit: freedom, practice and grace',
  'Most students want to change something about themselves (a temper, procrastination, a screen habit, a way they treat people) and have tried and failed. The Church’s teaching on freedom, virtue and grace gives an unusual answer: you change by practice, with help, and without despair after a failure. This lesson ties the Catechism on free will, the virtues, the struggle against sin and the sacraments into a practical method.',
  '1730-1738,1803-1811,2015,1865,2340-2342,1266',
  'Romans 7:15-25;Galatians 5:22-23;Luke 15:11-24;Proverbs 24:16',
  'free-will virtue grace sin',
  'What is one habit you wish you could change? What have you tried?',
  'Choose one habit to grow (not only one to stop). Write it as a clear action (“Read ten minutes before bed”). Then fill in four lines: the trigger (when it happens), the swap (what you do instead), the helper (a person who will check), and the reset (what you do when you fail). Try it for seven days.',
  'serenity',
  id='8-L1',
  hook='Ask a student to sign their name with their usual hand, then with the other hand. Ask: “Which one is easier? Why? How did your usual hand get that way?” Say, “Habits are just repeated choices that became easy.”',
  teach=[
    'Start with freedom. God made us free, and “freedom is the power, rooted in reason and will, to act or not to act” (CCC 1731). Freedom grows as we choose the good, and it can be weakened by habit and sin (CCC 1731–1738). Paul describes the struggle exactly: “I do not do the good I want, but the evil I do not want is what I do” (Romans 7:19). Every honest person knows that.',
    'The key idea is virtue: “a habitual and firm disposition to do the good” (CCC 1803). Virtues are not born; they are built by repeated choices, “by education, deliberate acts, and perseverance,” and strengthened by God’s grace (CCC 1810–1811). The Church names four central ones (prudence, justice, fortitude, temperance) and three given by God (faith, hope, charity). A habit is a path worn by walking; to change it, walk a new path until it is worn.',
    'Grace is real help. “The way of perfection passes by way of the Cross. There is no holiness without renunciation and spiritual battle” (CCC 2015), but we are not alone in it. In Baptism we receive God’s life (CCC 1266); in the Eucharist we are fed; in Confession we are forgiven and strengthened. Prayer asks for the help we cannot give ourselves. Self-mastery is “a long and exacting work” that is “never acquired once and for all” (CCC 2342).',
    'Failure is part of the story. “The righteous falls seven times and rises again” (Proverbs 24:16). The Prodigal Son’s father does not wait for a perfect speech. The key is the reset: not “I always fail” but “I start again today.” A practical method: choose one habit, make it small, attach it to a trigger, replace rather than only stop, tell a friend, and have a plan for what to do after a slip. Then repeat.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What is a habit that started small and became big, good or bad?',
    'Why is replacing a habit often easier than simply stopping it?',
    'Who could be your “helper” for a habit?',
    'What does it mean that we are never alone in the struggle?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What is a virtue?|A rule|A habitual and firm disposition to do the good|A feeling|A reward|1|CCC 1803.',
    'How are virtues built?|They appear|By repeated choices, practice and perseverance, helped by grace|Only by willpower|Only by punishment|1|CCC 1810–1811.',
    'What should you do after a slip?|Give up|Start again right away, ask for forgiveness, use your reset plan|Hide it|Blame others|1|Proverbs 24:16; Luke 15.',
  ],
  life='Run your habit plan for seven days. Mark each day with a check or a dot, no judgment. If you slip, write down what happened and what you would try next time, then start again. At the end of the week, tell your helper how it went.',
  saint='Thomas Aquinas: a young man whose family locked him up to stop him joining the Dominicans; he stuck to his choice and became one of the greatest teachers in the Church.',
  misc=[
    '“Willpower alone is enough.”',
    '“If I fail, it proves I cannot change.”',
    '“Grace means I do not have to try.”',
  ],
  deeper=['room:aug-confessions', 'room:desales-devout-life'],
  mem='“The righteous falls seven times and rises again.” (Proverbs 24:16)',
  assess='Ask each student to define virtue, name their trigger, swap, helper and reset, and describe what they will do after a slip.',
  script=dict(
    open='Ask a volunteer to sign their name with their usual hand, then with the other hand. Ask, “Which is easier? How did your usual hand get that way?” Say, “Habits are repeated choices that became easy.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “God made you free. The Catechism says freedom is the power to act or not to act. But freedom grows when we choose good and shrinks when habits and sin pull us. Paul says, ‘I do not do the good I want, but the evil I do not want.’ Everyone knows that feeling. It is not a sign you are bad. It is part of being human.”',
      'Say: “The word to know is virtue: a steady habit of doing good. Virtues are built by repeated choices. A habit is a path worn by walking. To change a path, walk a new one until it is worn. That is slow, and it helps to make the change small and specific.”',
      'Say: “And you are not alone. The Catechism says there is no holiness without spiritual battle, but God helps. In Baptism we receive his life. In the Eucharist we are fed. In Confession we are forgiven and strengthened. Prayer asks for what we cannot give ourselves. Self-mastery is a long and exacting work, never done once and for all.”',
      'Say: “Failure is part of the story. Proverbs says the righteous person falls seven times and rises again. The father in the story of the Prodigal Son runs to his son. The key is the reset: not ‘I always fail,’ but ‘I start again today.’ Here is a plan: choose one small habit, tie it to a trigger, swap rather than only stop, tell a friend, and plan your reset.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What is a habit you want to start, not just stop?',
      'What is your trigger? What will you swap in?',
      'Who will be your helper?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Hand out a habit card with four lines: Trigger, Swap, Helper, Reset.',
      'Fill it in for one habit, written as an action.',
      'Tell your helper (a partner for now) and agree on a time to check in.',
    ],
    close='Pray the serenity prayer together. Say, “Small, specific, with help, and start again.”'),
  tryit=['Write one habit as a clear action.', 'Fill in trigger, swap, helper, reset.', 'Tell your helper.'],
  saintStory='Thomas was born around 1225 in the castle of Roccasecca, in southern Italy. His family was powerful, and expected him to become the abbot of the great monastery of Monte Cassino. At about nineteen, he decided instead to join the Dominicans, a new order of poor preachers and students.\nHis family was furious. While Thomas was traveling to Paris, his brothers seized him on the road and shut him up in the family castle for about a year. They tried to talk him out of it, then sent a woman to tempt him; he drove her out with a burning log from the fire. His sisters are said to have helped him escape by lowering him from a window in a basket.\nHe returned to the Dominicans and studied under Albert the Great. His classmates called him the “dumb ox” because he was big and quiet. Albert said, “One day the bellowing of this ox will fill the world.” Thomas wrote the Summa Theologiae and many other works, and taught in Paris and Italy. He died in 1274 on his way to the Council of Lyon, and was canonized in 1323.',
),

L('Prayer you can actually do',
  'Many students say they do not know how to pray, or that prayer is boring, or that they forget. The Church’s tradition offers more variety and ease than students expect: short vocal prayers, reading Scripture with attention, talking to God in your own words, and simply being quiet. This lesson ties together the Catechism’s teaching on the forms of prayer, the times of prayer and the Mass into a simple rhythm a thirteen-year-old can keep.',
  '2558-2565,2623-2643,2697-2699,2705-2708,2709-2719,2650-2651',
  'Luke 11:1-4;Matthew 6:5-8;Psalm 63:1-8;Luke 10:38-42',
  'prayer adoration petition thanksgiving',
  'When you have prayed and it felt real, what was happening around you?',
  'Make your own “prayer rhythm” card with four times: morning (one short prayer), midday (one sentence), evening (a three-part look back: thanks, sorry, please) and weekly (Mass). For each, write what you will say or do and how long it takes. Try it for a week.',
  'morning-offering',
  id='8-L2',
  hook='Ask: “How do you talk to your best friend? Do you only use set words? Do you ever just sit?” Then say, “Prayer is talking and listening to someone who already loves you.”',
  teach=[
    'The Catechism begins with a simple description: “Prayer is the raising of one’s mind and heart to God or the requesting of good things from God” (CCC 2559). Prayer is relationship before it is technique. And it starts with God: “God thirsts that we may thirst for him” (CCC 2560). Prayer is a response to an invitation.',
    'There are different forms, and variety helps. The Catechism names five: blessing and adoration, petition, intercession, thanksgiving and praise (CCC 2626–2643). They are ways of saying: you are great; I need you; I pray for others; thank you; glory to you. There are also three expressions: vocal (words), meditation (thinking with God over Scripture or a scene), and contemplative (simply being with God) (CCC 2700–2719). Most people begin with vocal prayer and a little meditation, and that is fine.',
    'Teresa of Avila said that mental prayer is “nothing else than a close sharing between friends; it means taking time frequently to be alone with him who we know loves us” (quoted in CCC 2709). Five minutes of that, honestly, is better than twenty of just reciting. Use a Gospel passage: read slowly, imagine the scene, notice what stands out, talk to Jesus about it, and finish with a simple resolution.',
    'Rhythm is the secret. The Church recommends prayer at set times so that it does not rely on mood (CCC 2697–2699). A morning offering to start; a sentence at midday; an evening look back at the day with thanks, sorry and please; and Sunday Mass, the great prayer of the Church (CCC 2650–2651). Distraction and dryness are normal; you simply come back. Prayer is a habit of love, and it works like friendship: it grows by showing up.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What is the difference between saying prayers and praying?',
    'Which of the five forms (adoration, petition, intercession, thanksgiving, praise) comes most easily to you?',
    'What time of day would you actually keep?',
    'What do you do when your mind wanders?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'How does the Catechism describe prayer?|A list of words|The raising of one’s mind and heart to God|A feeling|A duty only for priests|1|CCC 2559.',
    'What does Teresa of Avila say mental prayer is?|A close sharing between friends|A hard exam|A silent exercise only|A rule|0|CCC 2709.',
    'What do we do when we are distracted?|Give up|Simply come back|Pray longer|Wait for the feeling|1|CCC 2729.',
  ],
  life='For one week keep your prayer rhythm card. Morning offering (one minute), a sentence at midday, an evening look back (thanks, sorry, please), and Mass on Sunday. At the end of the week note what worked and what to change.',
  saint='Teresa of Avila: a Spanish nun who spent twenty years praying badly, then taught thousands of people how to pray well.',
  misc=[
    '“Prayer only works if I feel something.”',
    '“Reading a prayer is not real prayer.”',
    '“I am too distracted to pray.”',
  ],
  deeper=['room:teresa-castle', 'room:ignatius-exercises'],
  mem='“Lord, teach us to pray.” (Luke 11:1)',
  assess='Ask each student to name the five forms of prayer, describe their own rhythm card, and say what they do when they are distracted.',
  script=dict(
    open='Ask, “How do you talk to your best friend? Do you only use set words? Do you ever just sit?” Let them answer. Say, “Prayer is talking and listening to someone who already loves you.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “The Catechism says prayer is raising your mind and heart to God. It begins with God, who thirsts for us. Prayer is not a performance; it is an answer to an invitation. So the question is not ‘Am I good at it?’ but ‘Will I show up?’”',
      'Say: “There are five forms: adoration, petition, intercession, thanksgiving and praise. In plain words: you are great; I need you; help them; thank you; glory to you. Most of us use only petition. Try mixing them. There are also three kinds: words, meditation, and just being with God. Start with words and a little meditation.”',
      'Say: “Teresa of Avila said mental prayer is just a close sharing between friends, taking time to be alone with someone who loves you. Here is a simple way: read a short Gospel passage slowly, imagine the scene, notice what stands out, talk to Jesus about it, and finish with one small resolution. Five minutes, honestly.”',
      'Say: “Rhythm is the secret. Morning offering to start. One sentence at midday. In the evening: thanks, sorry, please. And Mass on Sunday, the great prayer of the Church. Your mind will wander. When it does, simply come back. That is the practice. Friendship grows by showing up.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'Which form of prayer do you use most? Which is hardest?',
      'When in your day could you actually keep a prayer?',
      'What helps you come back when you wander?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give each student a rhythm card with four times: morning, midday, evening, weekly.',
      'Write what you will say or do and how long.',
      'Try the morning prayer now, aloud, together.',
    ],
    close='Pray the morning offering together. Say, “Pray small, pray often, and keep coming back.”'),
  tryit=['Fill in a prayer rhythm card.', 'Choose one time you will keep.', 'Pray it once now.'],
  saintStory='Teresa de Cepeda y Ahumada was born in Ávila, Spain, in 1515, to a large family. When she was about twenty, she entered the Carmelite convent of the Incarnation. It was a comfortable place, with visitors, parties and little discipline, and she let herself be drawn into it. For almost twenty years she was, in her words, a poor pray-er, who often could not concentrate and sometimes dropped prayer altogether for months.\nIn her forties, after a deep conversion in front of an image of the wounded Christ, her prayer deepened. She began to have mystical experiences, and to think that a convent should be a quieter place of real prayer. In 1562 she founded a small reformed convent, St. Joseph’s in Ávila, in the face of opposition. Over the next twenty years she founded sixteen more, traveling across Spain on foot and by cart.\nShe wrote the Life, The Way of Perfection and The Interior Castle, teaching ordinary people how to pray. She was funny, practical, and fond of saying, “God deliver us from sullen saints.” She died in 1582, and in 1970 became the first woman named a Doctor of the Church.',
),

L('Loss, grief and what we believe about heaven',
  'Most students will lose a grandparent, a pet, a friend or a classmate before they graduate. The Church’s teaching on death, hope, heaven, purgatory and funerals offers concrete comfort and concrete things to do. It is honest about grief and explicit about what Christians believe happens next. This lesson prepares students to grieve well and to accompany others who are grieving.',
  '1005-1014,1020,1030-1032,1680-1690,2299,1024-1029',
  'John 11:17-44;Psalm 23;Revelation 21:1-5;1 Thessalonians 4:13-14',
  'eternal-life heaven purgatory hope',
  'Is there someone you have lost, or someone you worry about losing? What do you wish you could say?',
  'Write a short letter to someone you have lost (a person or a pet), saying what you are thankful for and what you miss. Then write a prayer for them (“Eternal rest grant unto them, O Lord”). Choose one thing you can do for them: light a candle, ask for a Mass, visit a grave, do an act of kindness in their name.',
  'eternal-rest',
  id='8-L3',
  hook='Read John 11:33–35: “Jesus wept.” Ask: “Why did Jesus cry when he knew he was about to raise Lazarus? What does it tell us about grief?”',
  teach=[
    'Start with honesty: grief is love with nowhere to go. Jesus wept at the tomb of his friend Lazarus, though he knew what he would do next (John 11:35). Tears are not a failure of faith. The Catechism says that death is a real loss that entered the world through sin (CCC 1008), and Christian hope does not make us stop missing people (see 1 Thessalonians 4:13: “that you may not grieve as others do who have no hope”).',
    'And the hope is concrete. Death is not the end of the person: “The Christian who unites his own death to that of Jesus views it as a step towards him and an entrance into everlasting life” (CCC 1010). At death the soul meets Christ (CCC 1021–1022). The Church teaches heaven as “life with Christ,” “perfect happiness,” “the fullness of communion with the Trinity” (CCC 1024–1026), and that the life we have now prepares us for it.',
    'The Church also says that we can still help those who have died. The Catechism teaches that some who die in God’s friendship are not yet fully ready, and they go through a final purification, which we call purgatory (CCC 1030–1031). So we pray for them and offer Mass for them (CCC 1032). That gives grief a task. In practice: ask for a Mass to be offered, say “Eternal rest grant unto them, O Lord,” light a candle, give alms in their name.',
    'The Church has rituals for this time: the vigil, the funeral Mass, and the committal (CCC 1680–1690). They exist to help people grieve together. If you are grieving, tell someone; do not carry it alone. If you notice a friend grieving, simply being there matters more than finding the right words. Send a card, sit with them, say their loved one’s name. And if grief feels too heavy, a parent, counselor or priest can help (CCC 2299).',
  ],
  talk=[
    'Why do you think Jesus wept even though he knew he would raise Lazarus?',
    'What helps you when you miss someone?',
    'What would you say to a friend whose grandparent died?',
    'What does it mean to pray for someone who has died?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'How does the Catechism describe heaven?|A cloud|Life with Christ, perfect happiness, fullness of communion with the Trinity|A place only for saints|An idea|1|CCC 1024–1026.',
    'Why does the Church pray for the dead?|It is a custom|Because some who die in God’s friendship still need purification and our prayers help|They have been forgotten|It earns merit|1|CCC 1030–1032.',
    'Is grief a lack of faith?|Yes|No, Jesus wept at the death of his friend|Only for adults|Only if it is long|1|John 11:35.',
  ],
  life='This week, do one act for someone who has died: ask for a Mass to be said, light a candle, say “Eternal rest” with their name, or visit a grave. And do one act for someone grieving: a card, a visit, an invitation.',
  saint='Monica: Augustine’s mother, who prayed for her son for years and asked only that he remember her at the altar of the Lord.',
  misc=[
    '“Christians should not be sad when someone dies.”',
    '“Prayers for the dead do not help.”',
    '“Heaven is just floating on clouds.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:purgatory', 'apol:heaven', 'apol:praying-for-the-dead'],
  mem='“Eternal rest grant unto them, O Lord, and let perpetual light shine upon them.”',
  assess='Ask each student to explain what the Church teaches about heaven and purgatory, and to name one thing they can do for someone who has died and one for someone grieving.',
  script=dict(
    open='Read John 11:33–35 aloud. Ask, “Why did Jesus cry, when he knew he would raise Lazarus in a few minutes?” Take answers. Say, “Grief is love with nowhere to go. Jesus did not skip it.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “Start with honesty. Death is a real loss. Jesus wept. Tears are not a failure of faith. St. Paul says we should not grieve like people with no hope, not that we should not grieve. So miss them. That is love.”',
      'Say: “And there is real hope. For a Christian, death is a step toward Christ and into everlasting life. At death the soul meets Christ. The Catechism says heaven is life with Christ, perfect happiness, full communion with the Trinity. It is not clouds; it is being fully known and loved.”',
      'Say: “The Church also says we can help the dead. Some people die in God’s friendship but are not yet fully ready to see him, and they are purified. We call that purgatory. We pray for them and ask for Masses to be offered. This gives grief something to do. Say, ‘Eternal rest grant unto them, O Lord.’ Light a candle. Give something in their name.”',
      'Say: “The Church has rituals to help us: the vigil, the funeral Mass, the burial. They exist so we do not grieve alone. If you are grieving, tell someone. If you have a friend grieving, simply being with them matters more than finding the right words. Send a card. Say their loved one’s name. If grief gets too heavy, tell an adult.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'Who is someone you miss? What would you like to say to them?',
      'What helped you when you lost someone?',
      'What would you do for a friend who is grieving?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Write a short letter to someone you have lost: thanks, what you miss.',
      'Write the eternal rest prayer with their name.',
      'Choose one act: a Mass, a candle, a visit, a kindness in their name.',
    ],
    close='Pray the eternal rest together. Say, “Love goes on. Pray for them, and be there for the living.”'),
  tryit=['Write a short letter to someone you miss.', 'Write the eternal rest prayer with their name.', 'Pick one act to do this week.'],
  saintStory='Monica was born in 331 in Thagaste, in North Africa, and was married young to Patricius, a pagan official with a quick temper. She lived patiently with him for years, and he became a Christian shortly before he died. They had three children, of whom Augustine was the eldest. Augustine turned away from the faith as a young man, and took up with the Manichees and with a concubine.\nMonica wept and prayed for him for seventeen years. A bishop she asked for help told her, “It is not possible that the son of so many tears should perish.” When Augustine went to Italy, she followed him, and in Milan met Bishop Ambrose, who helped bring Augustine to the faith. In 387 she saw her son baptized.\nSoon after, in the port of Ostia, on the way back to Africa, she fell ill. Augustine wrote that she said, “Lay this body anywhere. Do not let the care of it trouble you. This only I ask, that you remember me at the altar of the Lord, wherever you may be.” She died at about 56. Augustine wrote about it in the Confessions, and her tomb is in Rome.',
),
])
